<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orlov revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1770</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Orlov Revolt · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orlov revolt was a Greek uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in 1770, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768–1774, encouraged by the Russian brothers Alexei and Fyodor Orlov, who landed with a small force. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> rose, but Russian support was weak, and Ottoman troops, many of them Albanian irregulars, crushed the revolt and ravaged the Peloponnese for years afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orlov-revolt.jpg" alt="Orlov revolt" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexikoua · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Russo-Turkish_War_(1768%E2%80%931774)_and_Orlov_Revolt_(1770).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
